WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Europe mandated biofuels to cut emissions and triggered Pete Forest clearing whose carbon

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.260
releases often exceeded the fossil fuels replaced.

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.660
This is unintended consequences, episode 117.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:17.560
The European Commission proposed the Renewable Energy Directive in 2008.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.080
Member states had already committed under the Kyoto Protocol to reduce overall emissions.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.340
Transport remained one of the hardest sectors to decarbonize because electric vehicles

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.100
were still rare.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.760
air. Efficiency gains alone could not meet the targets. Policy makers therefore turned

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.840
to biofuels, which could be blended into existing diesel and gasoline supplies without

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.320
new engines or refueling infrastructure. The 2009 directive set a binding 10% target

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:52.640
for renewable energy and transport by 2020. At the time, European officials viewed vegetable

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:58.520
oil feed stocks as a readily available bridge technology that would also support rural economies

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:03.980
inside the union. The measure passed with broad support from environmental groups and agricultural

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:10.040
interests alike. The prevailing view was that imports would simply fill any gap without changing

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:16.260
land use patterns elsewhere. Models then in use treated global agricultural markets as elastic

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:21.440
enough to absorb the extra demand without pushing cultivation onto high-carbon soils.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:27.460
Once the directive took effect, member states began counting bio-fuel volumes toward their

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:32.680
national targets. They offered tax incentives or blending mandates to fuel suppliers.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.580
Early reports from the European Environment Agency noted rising biofuel consumption and

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.800
modest reported reductions in tailpipe carbon dioxide.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.860
Industry associations welcomed the certainty the targets provided. Several large European

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:54.000
oil companies announced multi-year supply contracts for palm oil and soy oil. By 2012,

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:59.560
12. The volume of palm oil imported into Europe for fuel use had risen sharply. Indonesia

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.640
and Malaysia supplied the majority of the increase. The surge in European demand raised

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:10.080
the price of vegetable oils worldwide. Plantation companies in Southeast Asia gained a strong

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:15.100
financial reason to expand onto peat soils that had previously been considered marginal.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:21.120
Clearing and draining those peat lands oxidized stored organic matter. This released large

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:26.420
quantities of carbon dioxide and methane. Life cycle studies conducted after the directive

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:31.480
was enforced found that, in many cases, the emissions from this land conversion exceeded

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:36.160
the emissions saved by replacing fossil diesel with the resulting biodiesel.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.040
Roads built to service new plantations opened adjacent forest areas to further conversion.

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.840
Displaced smallholders sometimes moved into remaining peat swamps. Because the directive

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.020
rewarded fuel suppliers for meeting volume targets rather than verified net emission reductions,

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:58.980
the market responded to the incentive that was actually in place. Indonesian and Malaysian

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:05.240
export statistics from the early 2010s show palm oil shipments to Europe growing several fold.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:10.960
Once the Pete was drained, subsidence and repeated fires added further emissions that continued

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:17.400
for years after the first harvest. By 2015 the European Commission acknowledged that conventional

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:22.720
biofuels could produce higher emissions than fossil fuels when land use change was included.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:27.920
Successive revisions to the directive introduced caps on crop-based biofuels,

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.500
and required reporting on indirect land use change. Some member states shifted subsidies

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:39.200
toward waste-based and advanced biofuels. Yet the earlier expansion of palm plantations had

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.840
already altered land use patterns across thousands of square kilometers. Indonesia and Malaysia

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:49.420
Asia responded by tightening their own peak protection rules, though enforcement remained

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:49.900
uneven.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.940
Today, the European Union maintains a declining cap on food-based biofuels, while still

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:58.840
pursuing lower carbon transport fuels through other routes.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:04.400
The plantations established during the high-demand years continue to produce palm oil for multiple

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:04.860
markets.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.880
When a policy sets a quantitative target without measuring the full system response, economic

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:13.680
actors will optimize for the metric that is rewarded.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.820
In this case, the rewarded metric was biofuel volume rather than verified net carbon reduction.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:25.360
Decision makers can reduce such surprises by testing whether the chosen indicator remains

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.680
aligned with the underlying goal once market forces scale.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.260
The same principle applies whenever governments or companies adopt simple numerical targets

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.840
for complex environmental or social outcomes.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:41.720
The loophole that matters most is usually the one the designers did not model.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:47.740
One practical test is to ask how the arithmetic changes if the cheapest available feedstock

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:49.700
moves from one continent to another.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:55.300
If the model cannot answer that question with existing data, the target is likely to produce

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.400
effects outside its original boundary.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.220
That's unintended consequences for today.

